  "content": {
    "id": "p4-q13-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "13",
    "prompt": "There are 10 boys and 10 girls practising for a traditional school dance. Each boy is to partner with one girl. To avoid height mismatches, each boy is given a number from 1 to 10 in rank order of height, as is each girl. A boy may partner with a girl if and only if their numbers differ by no more than 1. For example, Boy 4 may partner with Girls 3, 4 or 5, but not 2 or 6. How many ways can the boys and girls be partnered for this dance?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": {
        "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
        "version": 1,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "id": "p4-q13-short-result",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "89 ways.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p4-q13-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "The lowest-numbered remaining boy either partners with the girl of the same number or swaps partners with the next boy. Add the counts for these two cases.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Let $a_n$ count the valid pairings of $n$ boys and $n$ girls numbered from 1 to $n$.\n\nBoy 1 has two possible partners:\n\n• Girl 1: the remaining $n-1$ pairs can be formed in $a_{n-1}$ ways.\n\n• Girl 2: Girl 1 must then partner with Boy 2, leaving $a_{n-2}$ ways to pair the others.\n\nThese cases are mutually exclusive, so the addition principle gives\n$$a_n=a_{n-1}+a_{n-2}.$$\nThere is one pairing for one boy and one girl, and two pairings for two boys and two girls.\n$$\\begin{align*}a_1&=1,\\qquad a_2=2\\\\a_3&=2+1=3\\\\a_4&=3+2=5\\\\a_5&=5+3=8\\\\a_6&=8+5=13\\\\a_7&=13+8=21\\\\a_8&=21+13=34\\\\a_9&=34+21=55\\\\a_{10}&=55+34=89.\\end{align*}$$\nThere are 89 ways to partner the boys and girls.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 58
  },
